Hidradenitis Suppurativa
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Recurrent painful flexural nodules, Draining sinus tracts, Hypertrophic scarring

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-025
Reason for Visit: Acute, agonizing exacerbation of a recurrent axillary mass, severely restricting arm mobility and causing significant distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with an agonizing, rapidly enlarging mass in the axilla causing severe localized pain that restricted all arm mobility. The patient was unable to dress or sleep. Urgent assessment was necessary to differentiate an acute staphylococcal abscess requiring immediate surgical drainage from a severe inflammatory flare of underlying disease, and to provide potent immediate analgesia and targeted anti-inflammatory/antibiotic therapy before the next routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My armpit is completely swollen with these agonizing lumps that are bursting and draining. I can't lift my arm at all because the pain is so sharp."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a 5-year history of recurrent painful, deep-seated inflammatory nodules in the bilateral axillae and groin. For the past 48 hours, they have experienced an acute, severe exacerbation in the right axilla. The area features multiple nodules that have coalesced into a large, exquisitely tender inflammatory mass. The patient notes the formation of sterile abscesses that spontaneously rupture, producing a purulent, malodorous discharge. The severe localized pain has severely restricted mobility of the right shoulder. The patient reports extreme psychosocial distress and frustration with the recurrent nature of the condition, severely impacting their quality of life.
Functional Status: Functionally impaired due to severe mechanical pain in the dominant arm.
Social/Safety Concerns: High psychosocial distress; poor quality of life due to chronic pain and malodorous discharge.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 98 | Temp: 37.4 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 92 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, sitting with the right arm rigidly abducted to avoid skin contact and friction in the axilla.
Physical Exam: Examination of the right axilla reveals a complex, severely inflamed 6x8 cm plaque. It is characterized by multiple deep, erythematous, tender nodules, several of which are actively weeping purulent, malodorous fluid. There is clear evidence of chronic disease, including the development of chronic draining sinus tracts (tunnels) and severe hypertrophic scarring with double-comedone formation. The left axilla shows similar but quiescent scarring. Despite the purulence, there is no rapidly spreading, hot cellulitic erythema extending down the arm or across the chest wall, and the patient lacks high fever or rigors.
Environmental Observations: Patient is a smoker (cigarettes visible).
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient uses intermittent topical clindamycin; no current systemic therapy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hidradenitis Suppurativa (Hurley Stage II/III) - Status: Acute, severe inflammatory flare
Differentials:

Primary Staphylococcal Abscess (Furuncle/Carbuncle) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of chronic double-comedones, hypertrophic scarring, and interconnected sinus tracts, which define HS and distinguish it from a simple primary bacterial infection.

Lymphogranuloma Venereum / Lymphadenitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The chronicity of the lesions, bilateral symmetrical presentation history, and superficial dermal/subcutaneous involvement rather than true deep lymph node pathology.

Epidermoid Cyst (Ruptured) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The multiplicity of the lesions and the chronic sinus tract formation in a classic apocrine-gland distribution.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to chronicity and psychosocial impact)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, agonizing flare of long-standing Hidradenitis Suppurativa. The complex architecture of the lesions (sinus tracts, scarring) confirms the diagnosis. Triage correctly recognized the severe pain and loss of mobility as a crisis requiring urgent intervention. Out-of-hours clinical evaluation was vital to determine if immediate surgical incision and drainage was appropriate; however, given the diffuse inflammatory nature of HS and interconnected sinus tracts, bedside I&D is often contraindicated and ineffective. Instead, urgent intervention provided immediate potent systemic analgesia, intralesional corticosteroids (if available) or systemic anti-inflammatory antibiotics to rapidly calm the intense immune response, safely managing the severe pain crisis without inappropriate surgical trauma.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Doxycycline 100mg BD for 4 weeks (for its anti-inflammatory properties in HS). Administered a stat dose of strong oral analgesia (Oxycodone 5mg) to break the acute pain cycle, and prescribed regular Paracetamol/Ibuprofen. Applied a highly absorbent, non-adherent foam dressing to manage the malodorous exudate and reduce friction.
Positive Behavioral Change: By taking the Doxycycline strictly twice a day with a large glass of water, you will utilize its strong anti-inflammatory effects to shrink the painful nodules from the inside out, reducing the pressure and pain. By keeping this thick foam dressing in place, you will physically stop the skin from rubbing together, which is the primary mechanical trigger causing these lumps to flare up and burst.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral generated for a specialist Dermatology review.
DME/Safety (Optional): Absorbent foam dressings recommended.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review in 3 days to assess pain control and arrange long-term immunomodulatory management.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness suddenly starts spreading rapidly down your arm or across your chest, if you develop a fever over 38.5C with uncontrollable shivering, or if the pain becomes completely intolerable, present to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: HS is a chronic, complex inflammatory disease of the hair follicles, not an infection caused by poor hygiene.
Clinical Course: The acute throbbing pain and swelling should slowly improve over the next 5-7 days on the medication. Long-term, specialist treatment is required to prevent future scarring.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor for signs of true spreading bacterial infection (rapidly advancing redness, high fever).
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: This is an autoimmune-like reaction where your body severely attacks its own hair follicles in areas of friction. The long-course antibiotic is actually being used to calm down your immune system, not just to kill bacteria.
Actionable Steps: Smoking is a major trigger for this disease; reducing or quitting will dramatically lessen the severity of future flares. Wear loose, unrestrictive clothing to minimize armpit friction.

